      "seed": 1422193076,
      "version": 394,
      "versionNonce": 1256496012,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789856145372,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "My understood about it (pt-br)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "My understood about it (pt-br)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "O7Sma3ztciZKrErBqgcUE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 84.15334217142731,
      "y": -178.04642789422022,
      "width": 947.1301798851187,
      "height": 111.83503431673623,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1959990452,
      "version": 1653,
      "versionNonce": 255928972,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789856906884,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)—the “D” in SOLID—dictates that high-level business rules should not depend directly on low-level technical details, such as\ndatabases, SDKs, external APIs, or email providers. Both should depend on an abstraction—typically an interface (e.g., in Java).\n\nInstead of `OrderService` instantiating `new MySqlOrderRepository()`, it should depend on something like `OrderRepository`; the concrete implementation is provided\nexternally, usually by Spring Boot via constructor injection.\n\n- High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.\n- Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions.",
      "fontSize": 11.183503431673623,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)—the “D” in SOLID—dictates that high-level business rules should not depend directly on low-level technical details, such as databases, SDKs, external APIs, or email providers. Both should depend on an abstraction—typically an interface (e.g., in Java).\n\nInstead of `OrderService` instantiating `new MySqlOrderRepository()`, it should depend on something like `OrderRepository`; the concrete implementation is provided externally, usually by Spring Boot via constructor injection.\n\n- High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.\n- Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
